For a fixed set $A$, write $\mathcal E(A)$ for its equivalence relations and
$\Pi(A)$ for its partitions. The two constructions can be pictured as follows:
$\Phi(R)=A/R$ takes classes, while $\Psi(P)={\sim_P}$ relates elements in the
same block.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (E) at (-2,0) {$\mathcal E(A)$};
\node (P) at (2,0) {$\Pi(A)$};
\draw[->] (E) to[bend left=28] node[above] {$\Phi$} (P);
\draw[->] (P) to[bend left=28] node[below] {$\Psi$} (E);
\node at (0,-1.8) {$\Psi(\Phi(R))=R\qquad\Phi(\Psi(P))=P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The right-to-left construction uses the unique block $B_a$ containing each
$a\in A$, with $[a]_P=B_a$ (steps 1.3 and 3.1). The round-trip identities are
steps 4.1 and 4.2, applied also to the class partition of $R$. This includes
$A=\varnothing$, whose partition and equivalence relation are both empty.
The rigorous proof above is independent of the diagram.
